WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.277
BERT-style models are a useful example of encoder-only NLP.

00:00:04.277 --> 00:00:11.602
During pretraining, BERT learned from context on both sides of a token through masked language modeling.

00:00:11.602 --> 00:00:19.307
A sentence such as “the student submitted the mask” asks the model to use surrounding words to predict

00:00:19.307 --> 00:00:23.967
a missing token. That training objective encourages strong contextual representations.

00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:31.202
For classification, a task-specific head can map a sequence representation to class labels.

00:00:31.202 --> 00:00:35.637
For named entity recognition, a token-classification head predicts a label for each token.

00:00:35.637 --> 00:00:40.237
For feature extraction, we can use the hidden states directly.

00:00:40.237 --> 00:00:47.572
For semantic similarity, plain BERT embeddings are usually not the best choice by themselves;

00:00:47.572 --> 00:00:55.232
Sentence Transformer models are specifically adapted so sentence vectors work well with similarity measures.

00:00:55.232 --> 00:01:01.412
The architecture family tells us the general capability, while the checkpoint’s training tells us what it is

00:01:01.412 --> 00:01:02.960
actually good at.
